/*
Time Complexity

Let:

L = length of the word

Σ = alphabet size (number of children, e.g. 26)

addWord

Time: O(L)

Space: O(L) (only for new nodes)

search

Best case (no .): O(L)

Worst case (all .): O(Σ^L)

Because each . can branch into all possible children

Space Complexity
Auxiliary space (DFS stack)

O(L) recursion depth

Trie storage

O(total characters stored)
→ worst case O(N × L)


Intuition

Store all words in a Trie.
While searching, move character by character:

  If the character is a normal letter, follow that path.
  
  If the character is a ., it can match any letter, so try all children recursively.

If any path successfully reaches the end of the word and lands on isEnd = true, the word exists.


Why DFS is needed

. creates multiple possible paths.
You must branch and explore all valid children, so recursion is unavoidable.
*/
class TrieNode{
    Map<Character,TrieNode> m = new HashMap<>();
    boolean isEnd = false;
}
class WordDictionary {
    TrieNode root;
    public WordDictionary() {
        root = new TrieNode();
    }

    public void addWord(String word) {
        TrieNode cur = root;
        for(char c:word.toCharArray()){
            if(!cur.m.containsKey(c)){
                cur.m.put(c,new TrieNode());
            }
            cur = cur.m.get(c);
        }
        cur.isEnd = true;
    }

    public boolean search(String word) {
        return dfs(word,0,root);
    }
    public boolean dfs(String word,int index,TrieNode root){
        TrieNode cur = root;
        for(int i=index;i<word.length();i++){
            char c = word.charAt(i);
            if(c=='.'){
                for(TrieNode child : cur.m.values()){
                    if(dfs(word,i+1,child)) return true;
                }
                return false;
            }
            else{
                if(!cur.m.containsKey(c))return false;
                cur = cur.m.get(c);
            }
            
        }
        return cur.isEnd;
    }
}
